The 1992 Census of Agriculture provides county-level evidence of substantial commercial fertilizer use in the agricultural region surrounding the Buena Vista–Talbot study area.

Marion County reported 1,680 farms using commercial fertilizer across 187,745 acres. Polk County reported 601 farms across 89,129 acres. Combined, the counties accounted for approximately 276,874 acres receiving commercial fertilizer.

Most reported fertilized acreage was classified as cropland excluding pastureland: 181,305 acres in Marion County and 85,359 acres in Polk County. Additional fertilizer application was reported on 6,440 acres of pastureland and rangeland in Marion County and 3,770 acres in Polk County.

The census also documented extensive acreage treated for weed, grass, or brush control, as well as insect and disease control.

These statistics establish that commercial fertilizer use was widespread in the two counties during the period immediately preceding the historical nitrate observations near Buena Vista–Talbot Roads. Combined with 1995 aerial imagery showing predominantly agricultural land use around the monitoring cluster, they provide relevant historical context for investigating agricultural nutrient inputs.

The census does not identify specific fertilizer products, nitrogen application rates, individual farms, or nutrient losses to groundwater and surface water. Consequently, these county-level figures cannot establish the source of nitrate at the monitoring locations.

Source: 1992 Census of Agriculture, Oregon County Data, Table 10, Agricultural Chemicals Used, Including Fertilizer: 1992 and 1987, printed page 220.
